// A binary operator over two DIFFERENT fixed widths is a clean type error, not
// an internal compiler error.
//
// A suffixed literal is exact-typed (design 53), so `2i16` is an `Int16` and
// nothing about `n * 2i16` on an `Int n` is a width the compiler gets to pick —
// the flexible-literal rule is for a BARE literal, which adopts the other
// operand's width. The typechecker let the mismatch through and codegen reached
// llvmlite with two different integer types:
//
//   internal compiler error at FILE:2:7 (BinaryOp): Type of #2 arg mismatch: i64 != i16
//
// (The breadcrumb is design 192 unit 2's; before it, the same failure said
// `internal compiler error:` and named no position at all.)
//
// FIXED by design 195 unit 2 — the operand-agreement funnel. The message names
// both types and both ways out: convert one operand, or drop the suffix and let
// the literal adopt. Found by tools/sawfuzz.py, whose literal-rewrite mutation
// suffixes a bare literal.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: requires both operands to have the same type
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: Int16
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: drop the `i16` suffix

func doubling(n: Int) -> Int {
    n * 2i16
}

func main() {
    print(doubling(21))
}
